our project  is an immaterial museum that means that  we appear  and disappear that focuses on  re-signifying  the relevance and the beauty of the  family kingdom  for berlin science week we will show two  videos that have been recently made for  the collection of the museo de longo  and have a discussion around them with  the artists and  the teams involved in the production of  both videos  both videos will give highlights on how  can we engage people with science  through art  our main objective as a project the  first one mapuku fool was premiered in  arts electronica  this last september and the second one  is a video of sign language  chilean sign language for mycological  concepts and will be premiered in berlin  science week  our core mission is to break down the  walls between  nature and culture to understand the  human being  as a part of this complex interdependent  system that is nature  i would say the biggest challenge at the  moment  when world politics are focusing a lot  on the economics  is to find funding for cultural projects  to make people  understand the relevance of culture and  art  for social transformation to survive  financially  in a country and in a society that  value less and less arts and culture  i would say that the biggest challenge  besides the funding  is that how can we make people relate to  science  in an autonomous and authentic way how  can we provide  effective tools for them so they can be  really interested in science and not  scared away because of the  cryptic language or complicated  visualizations  and i think we we chose art  as the medium because of its sensorial  and immersive possibilities  and that creates a personal relationship  between the object and the  spectator
 

